{
  "teamId": 158,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Milwaukee Brewers arrived with playoff ambitions and delivered, posting a 95-win season behind one of the best pitching staffs in baseball. They burst out of the gate in April, going 16-10 on the strength of timely hitting from unexpected sources — Travis Shaw's opening-day double setting the tone immediately. But May brought turbulence, a 13-15 stumble that tested the roster's resilience and raised early questions about whether the offense could carry its weight alongside a stellar 3.50 ERA rotation.\n\nThe answer came emphatically in June, when Milwaukee went 19-8 in their best month of the season. The catalyst was Willy Adames, acquired mid-May from Tampa Bay, who erupted with back-to-back clutch home runs that announced his arrival as the missing piece in the Brewers' lineup. The summer months of July and August saw the team sustain that momentum, going a combined 33-19, with Rowdy Tellez and Avisaíl García providing power at crucial moments and Adames continuing to deliver. Even when Josh Hader was touched for rare damaging blows, the Brewers' depth and dominant pitching kept them afloat.\n\nSeptember tightened things up at 14-12, but the division was already secure. Daniel Vogelbach's grand slam in early September was a highlight in an otherwise grinding final stretch, with Yelich's late-season double play symbolizing the offensive inconsistency that would haunt them. They entered October having proven themselves one of the NL's elite teams, only to run into a red-hot Atlanta Braves squad in the NLDS.\n\nThe postseason told a bittersweet story. Rowdy Tellez homered twice in the series and the Brewers showed fight, but Atlanta's lineup — led by Eddie Rosario and Joc Pederson at pivotal moments — simply would not relent. Even Josh Hader, nearly untouchable all year, surrendered a Freddie Freeman homer in the deciding game. The Brewers' season ended in the NLDS, three games to two, a painful finish for a team that had given Milwaukee so much to celebrate over 162 games.",
